    "RepairedText": "This message is in Adyghe (West Circassian). It’s a friendly, informal greeting that combines a standard hello with a check‑in and a request to keep things brief.\n\nHere’s a breakdown of what each part means:\n\n* Уимафэ шӏу – This is the Adyghe equivalent of “Hello” or “Good afternoon.” It literally translates to “Good day to you”.\n* о ухэт? – This is a dialectal way of asking “How are you?” or “How are you doing?”. The standard form is *Сыдэу ущыт?*, but *о ухэт?* is a common variant.\n* Кӏэкӏэу къэпӏошъущта? – This is the key part. Кӏэкӏэу means “briefly,” “in short,” or “in a nutshell,” and къэпӏошъущта? comes from the verb “to say” or “to tell.” Together, the phrase asks: “Can you put it briefly?” or “Can you give me the short version?”.\n\nIn short, the whole message can be understood as:\n> “Hello! How are you? Can you put it briefly (or tell me in a nutshell)?”\n\nIt sounds like a warm but direct way of greeting someone and asking them to get straight to the point.",
